(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("linux-internals",{"linux-internals/cgroup-v2-controllers-cpu-throttling-and-memory-limits":{"html":"\u003cp>Containers do not have their own kernel. Their limits are cgroup settings, and most mysterious slowness or restarts in Kubernetes can be explained by reading a few files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The unified hierarchy and delegation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>cpu.max, cpu.weight and CFS bandwidth throttling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>memory.max, memory.high and the cgroup OOM killer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>io controls and pids limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading the numbers: cpu.stat, memory.events and pressure files\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-hierarchy\">The hierarchy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In cgroup v2 there is one tree under /sys/fs/cgroup. A process belongs to one cgroup, and controllers such as cpu, memory, io and pids are enabled for a subtree. A parent limit bounds all its children. Systemd and container runtimes create the cgroups, and Kubernetes places each pod and container in the tree.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cpu-limits\">CPU limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>cpu.weight is a relative share used when the CPU is busy, which maps to a CPU request. cpu.max sets a hard quota per period, for example 50000 out of 100000 microseconds for half a CPU, which maps to a limit. When a group uses its quota early in the period, all its threads stop until the next period, even if other cores are idle. That is throttling.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-throttling-hurts-latency\">Why throttling hurts latency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A multi threaded service with a limit of one CPU can burn its quota in a few milliseconds across many threads, and then freeze for the rest of the period. Latency spikes appear with low average CPU. The counters nr_throttled and throttled_usec in cpu.stat show it. Raise or remove the limit, or reduce threads, and keep requests for scheduling.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-limits\">Memory limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>memory.max is a hard limit. If the group cannot reclaim enough page cache and the usage reaches it, the kernel kills a process in that group and the container is reported as out of memory killed. memory.high is a soft limit that makes the group reclaim and slow down before the hard limit. Page cache counts toward usage but can usually be reclaimed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-the-signals\">Reading the signals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>memory.events counts how many times the group hit high, max and oom_kill. cpu.pressure and memory.pressure report pressure stall information, the share of time tasks wait for the resource. io.max limits bandwidth and operations per device, and pids.max stops fork bombs. Compare these files with the application metrics to explain a symptom.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;cpu;cpu&gt;mem;mem&gt;psi\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">cgroup v2: controllers, CPU throttling and memory limits\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Container process\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cpu\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">cpu controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mem\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">memory controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"psi\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pressure files\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Container process to cpu controller; 2, cpu controller to memory controller; 3, memory controller to Pressure files.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Container process \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Many threads.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Burst.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cpu\">\u003cdt>cpu controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Quota per period.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Throttled.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mem\">\u003cdt>memory controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Limit and reclaim.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> OOM kill.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"psi\">\u003cdt>Pressure files \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stall time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unread.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-slow-responses-with-idle-cpus\">Incident: Slow responses with idle CPUs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Latency doubled in one service while node CPU showed forty percent idle.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/kubepods.slice/kubepods-burstable.slice/cpu.stat\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/fs/cgroup/kubepods.slice/kubepods-burstable.slice/cpu.stat\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">usage_usec 91823442\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nr_periods 120344\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nr_throttled 88211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">throttled_usec 40123311\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The service had a CPU limit of one core but ran sixteen threads, so it used its quota in the first few milliseconds of many periods and then stopped. More than seventy percent of periods were throttled. The team raised the limit, set the worker count to match the CPU request and added an alert on the throttled ratio. The latency returned to normal without adding nodes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading node CPU only and not cgroup throttling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting tight CPU limits on multi threaded services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring memory.events and oom_kill counts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating page cache as leaked memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting memory limits equal to the average use\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-hierarchy","text":"The hierarchy"},{"id":"cpu-limits","text":"CPU limits"},{"id":"why-throttling-hurts-latency","text":"Why throttling hurts latency"},{"id":"memory-limits","text":"Memory limits"},{"id":"reading-the-signals","text":"Reading the signals"},{"id":"incident-slow-responses-with-idle-cpus","text":"Incident: Slow responses with idle CPUs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Control Group v2","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html"},{"t":"CFS bandwidth control","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/scheduler/sched-bwc.html"},{"t":"Pressure stall information","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/accounting/psi.html"}],"text":"Containers do not have their own kernel. Their limits are cgroup settings, and most mysterious slowness or restarts in Kubernetes can be explained by reading a few files. What you will learn The unified hierarchy and delegation cpu.max, cpu.weight and CFS bandwidth throttling memory.max, memory.high and the cgroup OOM killer io controls and pids limits Reading the numbers: cpu.stat, memory.events and pressure files The hierarchy In cgroup v2 there is one tree under /sys/fs/cgroup. A process belongs to one cgroup, and controllers such as cpu, memory, io and pids are enabled for a subtree. A parent limit bounds all its children. Systemd and container runtimes create the cgroups, and Kubernetes places each pod and container in the tree. CPU limits cpu.weight is a relative share used when the CPU is busy, which maps to a CPU request. cpu.max sets a hard quota per period, for example 50000 out of 100000 microseconds for half a CPU, which maps to a limit. When a group uses its quota early in the period, all its threads stop until the next period, even if other cores are idle. That is throttling. Why throttling hurts latency A multi threaded service with a limit of one CPU can burn its quota in a few milliseconds across many threads, and then freeze for the rest of the period. Latency spikes appear with low average CPU. The counters nr_throttled and throttled_usec in cpu.stat show it. Raise or remove the limit, or reduce threads, and keep requests for scheduling. Memory limits memory.max is a hard limit. If the group cannot reclaim enough page cache and the usage reaches it, the kernel kills a process in that group and the container is reported as out of memory killed. memory.high is a soft limit that makes the group reclaim and slow down before the hard limit. Page cache counts toward usage but can usually be reclaimed. Reading the signals memory.events counts how many times the group hit high, max and oom_kill. cpu.pressure and memory.pressure report pressure stall information, the share of time tasks wait for the resource. io.max limits bandwidth and operations per device, and pids.max stops fork bombs. Compare these files with the application metrics to explain a symptom. cgroup v2: controllers, CPU throttling and memory limits Flow in order: 1, Container process to cpu controller; 2, cpu controller to memory controller; 3, memory controller to Pressure files. Service Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Container process Service What it does: Many threads. If it fails or is weak: Burst. cpu controller Step What it does: Quota per period. If it fails or is weak: Throttled. memory controller Step What it does: Limit and reclaim. If it fails or is weak: OOM kill. Pressure files Observability What it does: Stall time. If it fails or is weak: Unread. Incident: Slow responses with idle CPUs Incident Latency doubled in one service while node CPU showed forty percent idle. $ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/kubepods.slice/kubepods-burstable.slice/cpu.stat usage_usec 91823442 nr_periods 120344 nr_throttled 88211 throttled_usec 40123311 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The service had a CPU limit of one core but ran sixteen threads, so it used its quota in the first few milliseconds of many periods and then stopped. More than seventy percent of periods were throttled. The team raised the limit, set the worker count to match the CPU request and added an alert on the throttled ratio. The latency returned to normal without adding nodes. Common mistakes Reading node CPU only and not cgroup throttling Setting tight CPU limits on multi threaded services Ignoring memory.events and oom_kill counts Treating page cache as leaked memory Setting memory limits equal to the average use"},"linux-internals/the-cpu-scheduler-run-queues-numa-and-pinning":{"html":"\u003cp>A server with free CPU can still be slow because the work is on the wrong cores, the memory is on the wrong socket or interrupts are fighting the application. These are scheduler and topology problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Run queues, load average and what they count\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scheduling classes, priorities and nice values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Interrupts and softirq on the same cores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>NUMA: local and remote memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pinning, isolation and when to avoid it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"run-queues-and-load\">Run queues and load\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each CPU has a queue of runnable tasks. The load average counts tasks that are runnable or in uninterruptible sleep, so a high load can mean CPU contention or blocked disk waits. Compare it with CPU use, the run queue length from vmstat and the pressure information to decide which one it is.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"classes-and-priority\">Classes and priority\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Normal tasks use the fair scheduler, which shares CPU by weight, and nice values change the weight. Real time classes run before normal tasks and can starve them, so use them with care. Container CPU requests become weights, so a busy node divides time in proportion to requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interrupts\">Interrupts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Network cards and disks raise interrupts that are handled on some cores. If all network interrupts land on one core, that core can saturate while others idle, and softirq time shows high in the CPU breakdown. Receive side scaling and interrupt affinity spread the work across cores.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"numa\">NUMA\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>On a multi socket server, each socket has local memory, and access to the other socket is slower. A process that runs on one socket but has its memory on another pays that cost on every access. Tools such as numactl and numastat show placement. Databases and in memory caches are most sensitive to this.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pinning\">Pinning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pinning a process to specific cores, or isolating cores from the general scheduler, reduces jitter for latency critical work such as packet processing. It also prevents the scheduler from using idle cores, so a pinned job can wait while others sit idle. Measure before and after, and avoid pinning general purpose services.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"task&gt;sched;sched&gt;irq;irq&gt;numa\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The CPU scheduler, run queues, NUMA and pinning\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"task\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runnable tasks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sched\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scheduler\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"irq\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Interrupt handling\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"numa\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">NUMA memory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Runnable tasks to Scheduler; 2, Scheduler to Interrupt handling; 3, Interrupt handling to NUMA memory.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"task\">\u003cdt>Runnable tasks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Queue per core.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pile up.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sched\">\u003cdt>Scheduler \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Weights.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Starved.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"irq\">\u003cdt>Interrupt handling \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One core busy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skewed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"numa\">\u003cdt>NUMA memory \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Local access.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Remote.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-busy-core-fifteen-idle\">Incident: One busy core, fifteen idle\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Network throughput stopped growing on a fast server although total CPU use was low.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mpstat -P ALL 1 1 | sort -k12 -n | head -3\ngrep eth0 /proc/interrupts | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>mpstat -P ALL 1 1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -k12 -n | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Average:   3    0.00    0.00    1.00    0.00    9.00   88.00    0.00    0.00    0.00    2.00\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep eth0 /proc/interrupts | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 64:  91823442   0   0   0   IR-PCI-MSI eth0-TxRx-0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>All interrupts for the network card were handled by core zero, which spent nearly all its time in softirq while other cores idled. The packet rate was limited by that single core. The team enabled multiple receive queues, spread their interrupt affinity across cores on the same NUMA node as the card and confirmed that throughput scaled with the number of queues.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading load average as CPU use only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring softirq time in the CPU breakdown\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running memory heavy services across sockets without checking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pinning general services to cores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using real time priority to fix a slow service\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"run-queues-and-load","text":"Run queues and load"},{"id":"classes-and-priority","text":"Classes and priority"},{"id":"interrupts","text":"Interrupts"},{"id":"numa","text":"NUMA"},{"id":"pinning","text":"Pinning"},{"id":"incident-one-busy-core-fifteen-idle","text":"Incident: One busy core, fifteen idle"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Scheduler documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/scheduler/index.html"},{"t":"NUMA memory policy","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/numa_memory_policy.html"},{"t":"Linux performance","u":"https://www.brendangregg.com/linuxperf.html"}],"text":"A server with free CPU can still be slow because the work is on the wrong cores, the memory is on the wrong socket or interrupts are fighting the application. These are scheduler and topology problems. What you will learn Run queues, load average and what they count Scheduling classes, priorities and nice values Interrupts and softirq on the same cores NUMA: local and remote memory Pinning, isolation and when to avoid it Run queues and load Each CPU has a queue of runnable tasks. The load average counts tasks that are runnable or in uninterruptible sleep, so a high load can mean CPU contention or blocked disk waits. Compare it with CPU use, the run queue length from vmstat and the pressure information to decide which one it is. Classes and priority Normal tasks use the fair scheduler, which shares CPU by weight, and nice values change the weight. Real time classes run before normal tasks and can starve them, so use them with care. Container CPU requests become weights, so a busy node divides time in proportion to requests. Interrupts Network cards and disks raise interrupts that are handled on some cores. If all network interrupts land on one core, that core can saturate while others idle, and softirq time shows high in the CPU breakdown. Receive side scaling and interrupt affinity spread the work across cores. NUMA On a multi socket server, each socket has local memory, and access to the other socket is slower. A process that runs on one socket but has its memory on another pays that cost on every access. Tools such as numactl and numastat show placement. Databases and in memory caches are most sensitive to this. Pinning Pinning a process to specific cores, or isolating cores from the general scheduler, reduces jitter for latency critical work such as packet processing. It also prevents the scheduler from using idle cores, so a pinned job can wait while others sit idle. Measure before and after, and avoid pinning general purpose services. The CPU scheduler, run queues, NUMA and pinning Flow in order: 1, Runnable tasks to Scheduler; 2, Scheduler to Interrupt handling; 3, Interrupt handling to NUMA memory. Step Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Runnable tasks Step What it does: Queue per core. If it fails or is weak: Pile up. Scheduler Step What it does: Weights. If it fails or is weak: Starved. Interrupt handling Service What it does: One core busy. If it fails or is weak: Skewed. NUMA memory Data store What it does: Local access. If it fails or is weak: Remote. Incident: One busy core, fifteen idle Incident Network throughput stopped growing on a fast server although total CPU use was low. $ mpstat -P ALL 1 1 | sort -k12 -n | head -3 Average: 3 0.00 0.00 1.00 0.00 9.00 88.00 0.00 0.00 0.00 2.00 $ grep eth0 /proc/interrupts | head -2 64: 91823442 0 0 0 IR-PCI-MSI eth0-TxRx-0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) All interrupts for the network card were handled by core zero, which spent nearly all its time in softirq while other cores idled. The packet rate was limited by that single core. The team enabled multiple receive queues, spread their interrupt affinity across cores on the same NUMA node as the card and confirmed that throughput scaled with the number of queues. Common mistakes Reading load average as CPU use only Ignoring softirq time in the CPU breakdown Running memory heavy services across sockets without checking Pinning general services to cores Using real time priority to fix a slow service"},"linux-internals/virtual-memory-page-tables-huge-pages-swap-and-pressure":{"html":"\u003cp>Free memory is the wrong number to watch. Linux uses spare memory as cache on purpose, so the useful questions are how much can be reclaimed and how long tasks wait for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Virtual addresses, page tables and page faults\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The page cache and reclaim\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Transparent huge pages and explicit huge pages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Swap, swappiness and zswap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Memory pressure and what to alert on\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"addresses-and-faults\">Addresses and faults\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each process sees its own virtual address space. The kernel maps pages to physical memory through page tables and fills them on demand. A minor page fault maps a page that is already in memory, and a major fault must read from disk. A rising rate of major faults means the working set no longer fits in memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cache-and-reclaim\">Cache and reclaim\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Spare memory is used for the page cache, which holds file data. Clean cache can be dropped at once, so it counts as available. When memory is low, the kernel reclaims cache first and then anonymous pages by swapping them out. The MemAvailable value estimates how much can be used without swapping.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"huge-pages\">Huge pages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Normal pages are 4 kilobytes. Huge pages of 2 megabytes reduce page table overhead and translation misses, which helps large heaps in databases and the Java virtual machine. Transparent huge pages try to do this automatically, but the background compaction can cause latency spikes, so some databases advise turning them off. Explicit huge pages are reserved ahead of time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"swap\">Swap\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Swap lets the kernel move rarely used anonymous pages out, which frees memory for cache. A little swap in use is normal. Heavy swapping while the system waits is the problem, shown by high swap in and out rates. The swappiness setting changes the balance between reclaiming cache and swapping. Compressed memory swap can reduce disk use.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pressure\">Pressure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pressure stall information reports the share of time tasks stalled waiting for memory. Alert on sustained memory pressure, major fault rates and out of memory kills, not on low free memory. In Kubernetes, remember that page cache counts in the container memory figure but can be reclaimed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"proc&gt;cache;cache&gt;swap;swap&gt;psi\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Virtual memory: page tables, huge pages, swap and pressure\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Process\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cache\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Page cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"swap\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Swap\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"psi\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pressure signal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Process to Page cache; 2, Page cache to Swap; 3, Swap to Pressure signal.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proc\">\u003cdt>Process \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Virtual pages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fault storm.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cache\">\u003cdt>Page cache \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reclaimable.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Dropped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"swap\">\u003cdt>Swap \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Anonymous pages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Thrash.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"psi\">\u003cdt>Pressure signal \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stall time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-database-that-stalled-every-few-minutes\">Incident: The database that stalled every few minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Query latency spiked for a few seconds at regular intervals on a database host with plenty of free memory.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled\ngrep -E &quot;thp_collapse_alloc|compact_stall&quot; /proc/vmstat\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[always] madvise never\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;thp_collapse_alloc|compact_stall&quot;\u003c/span> /proc/vmstat\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">thp_collapse_alloc 18211\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">compact_stall 4410\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Transparent huge pages were set to always, and the kernel stalled the database while it compacted memory to build huge pages. The stall counters matched the latency spikes. The team set the mode to madvise as the database vendor recommends, reserved explicit huge pages for the shared buffer and watched the compaction stall counter. The spikes disappeared.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Alerting on free memory instead of pressure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling swap with no plan for memory pressure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving transparent huge pages on for latency critical databases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing page cache with a leak\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring major page fault rates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"addresses-and-faults","text":"Addresses and faults"},{"id":"cache-and-reclaim","text":"Cache and reclaim"},{"id":"huge-pages","text":"Huge pages"},{"id":"swap","text":"Swap"},{"id":"pressure","text":"Pressure"},{"id":"incident-the-database-that-stalled-every-few-minutes","text":"Incident: The database that stalled every few minutes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Memory management concepts","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/concepts.html"},{"t":"Transparent hugepage support","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/transhuge.html"},{"t":"Pressure stall information","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/accounting/psi.html"}],"text":"Free memory is the wrong number to watch. Linux uses spare memory as cache on purpose, so the useful questions are how much can be reclaimed and how long tasks wait for it. What you will learn Virtual addresses, page tables and page faults The page cache and reclaim Transparent huge pages and explicit huge pages Swap, swappiness and zswap Memory pressure and what to alert on Addresses and faults Each process sees its own virtual address space. The kernel maps pages to physical memory through page tables and fills them on demand. A minor page fault maps a page that is already in memory, and a major fault must read from disk. A rising rate of major faults means the working set no longer fits in memory. Cache and reclaim Spare memory is used for the page cache, which holds file data. Clean cache can be dropped at once, so it counts as available. When memory is low, the kernel reclaims cache first and then anonymous pages by swapping them out. The MemAvailable value estimates how much can be used without swapping. Huge pages Normal pages are 4 kilobytes. Huge pages of 2 megabytes reduce page table overhead and translation misses, which helps large heaps in databases and the Java virtual machine. Transparent huge pages try to do this automatically, but the background compaction can cause latency spikes, so some databases advise turning them off. Explicit huge pages are reserved ahead of time. Swap Swap lets the kernel move rarely used anonymous pages out, which frees memory for cache. A little swap in use is normal. Heavy swapping while the system waits is the problem, shown by high swap in and out rates. The swappiness setting changes the balance between reclaiming cache and swapping. Compressed memory swap can reduce disk use. Pressure Pressure stall information reports the share of time tasks stalled waiting for memory. Alert on sustained memory pressure, major fault rates and out of memory kills, not on low free memory. In Kubernetes, remember that page cache counts in the container memory figure but can be reclaimed. Virtual memory: page tables, huge pages, swap and pressure Flow in order: 1, Process to Page cache; 2, Page cache to Swap; 3, Swap to Pressure signal. Service Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Process Service What it does: Virtual pages. If it fails or is weak: Fault storm. Page cache Data store What it does: Reclaimable. If it fails or is weak: Dropped. Swap Data store What it does: Anonymous pages. If it fails or is weak: Thrash. Pressure signal Observability What it does: Stall time. If it fails or is weak: Ignored. Incident: The database that stalled every few minutes Incident Query latency spiked for a few seconds at regular intervals on a database host with plenty of free memory. $ cat /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled [always] madvise never $ grep -E \"thp_collapse_alloc|compact_stall\" /proc/vmstat thp_collapse_alloc 18211 compact_stall 4410 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Transparent huge pages were set to always, and the kernel stalled the database while it compacted memory to build huge pages. The stall counters matched the latency spikes. The team set the mode to madvise as the database vendor recommends, reserved explicit huge pages for the shared buffer and watched the compaction stall counter. The spikes disappeared. Common mistakes Alerting on free memory instead of pressure Disabling swap with no plan for memory pressure Leaving transparent huge pages on for latency critical databases Confusing page cache with a leak Ignoring major page fault rates"},"linux-internals/the-block-layer-nvme-queues-schedulers-and-io-uring":{"html":"\u003cp>Disk latency is often the real cause of slow services, and the average hides it. Knowing the path from application write to durable storage explains most storage surprises.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Buffered writes, dirty pages and writeback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>fsync and what durability costs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The block layer, schedulers and queue depth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>NVMe multi queue design\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>io_uring and asynchronous IO\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"buffered-writes\">Buffered writes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A normal write goes into the page cache and returns quickly. The page is marked dirty, and the kernel writes it out later in the background. If the application crashes the data is safe in the cache, but if the machine loses power it can be lost. Limits on dirty memory force writers to wait when the device cannot keep up.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fsync\">fsync\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An fsync call asks for the data to reach stable storage and waits for the device to confirm. It is what databases use for durability, and its latency is what users feel at commit time. Many small fsync calls are slow on devices without a power protected cache. Group commits reduce the number of calls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"block-layer-and-schedulers\">Block layer and schedulers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The block layer turns file system requests into device requests, merges adjacent ones and orders them. For spinning disks a scheduler that sorts by position helps. For fast flash devices, the none or a light scheduler is usual, because the device handles ordering itself. Choose per device and measure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"nvme-queues\">NVMe queues\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>NVMe devices have many hardware queues, and Linux maps them to CPUs so that submission does not take a shared lock. A deep queue gives high throughput, but also long waits for a request that arrives behind others. Latency under load, not peak throughput, matters for interactive services. Watch the queue depth and wait time with iostat.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"io-uring\">io_uring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>io_uring gives applications a pair of shared ring buffers to submit many IO requests and collect results with few system calls. It supports files, sockets and more, and can cut overhead for fast devices. It also widens the kernel attack surface, so some environments restrict it. Check that your runtime and security policy agree.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;wb;wb&gt;blk;blk&gt;dev\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The block layer: NVMe queues, schedulers and io_uring\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application write\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wb\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Writeback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"blk\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Block layer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">NVMe device\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application write to Writeback; 2, Writeback to Block layer; 3, Block layer to NVMe device.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application write \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Buffered.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost on crash.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wb\">\u003cdt>Writeback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Dirty pages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Throttle.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"blk\">\u003cdt>Block layer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Queues.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deep wait.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>NVMe device \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Durable.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow fsync.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-commits-that-took-a-second\">Incident: Commits that took a second\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A database showed slow commits while the average disk utilisation looked low.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"iostat -x 1 2 | tail -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>iostat -x 1 2 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Device  r/s   w/s  await  aqu-sz  %util\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nvme0n1 120   950  38.20  36.40   64.00\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The average utilisation looked acceptable, but the await value of thirty eight milliseconds and a queue of thirty six requests showed that writes waited behind a large background job that wrote at full speed. The team limited the job with the io controller, moved its output to another device and set an alert on await and queue depth. Commit latency fell to a few milliseconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Judging a disk by utilisation alone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring await and queue size\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running heavy batch writes beside a database\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming buffered write means durable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enabling io_uring without checking the security policy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"buffered-writes","text":"Buffered writes"},{"id":"fsync","text":"fsync"},{"id":"block-layer-and-schedulers","text":"Block layer and schedulers"},{"id":"nvme-queues","text":"NVMe queues"},{"id":"io-uring","text":"io_uring"},{"id":"incident-commits-that-took-a-second","text":"Incident: Commits that took a second"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Block layer documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/block/index.html"},{"t":"io_uring man page","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/io_uring.7.html"},{"t":"Linux performance","u":"https://www.brendangregg.com/linuxperf.html"}],"text":"Disk latency is often the real cause of slow services, and the average hides it. Knowing the path from application write to durable storage explains most storage surprises. What you will learn Buffered writes, dirty pages and writeback fsync and what durability costs The block layer, schedulers and queue depth NVMe multi queue design io_uring and asynchronous IO Buffered writes A normal write goes into the page cache and returns quickly. The page is marked dirty, and the kernel writes it out later in the background. If the application crashes the data is safe in the cache, but if the machine loses power it can be lost. Limits on dirty memory force writers to wait when the device cannot keep up. fsync An fsync call asks for the data to reach stable storage and waits for the device to confirm. It is what databases use for durability, and its latency is what users feel at commit time. Many small fsync calls are slow on devices without a power protected cache. Group commits reduce the number of calls. Block layer and schedulers The block layer turns file system requests into device requests, merges adjacent ones and orders them. For spinning disks a scheduler that sorts by position helps. For fast flash devices, the none or a light scheduler is usual, because the device handles ordering itself. Choose per device and measure. NVMe queues NVMe devices have many hardware queues, and Linux maps them to CPUs so that submission does not take a shared lock. A deep queue gives high throughput, but also long waits for a request that arrives behind others. Latency under load, not peak throughput, matters for interactive services. Watch the queue depth and wait time with iostat. io_uring io_uring gives applications a pair of shared ring buffers to submit many IO requests and collect results with few system calls. It supports files, sockets and more, and can cut overhead for fast devices. It also widens the kernel attack surface, so some environments restrict it. Check that your runtime and security policy agree. The block layer: NVMe queues, schedulers and io_uring Flow in order: 1, Application write to Writeback; 2, Writeback to Block layer; 3, Block layer to NVMe device. Service Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application write Service What it does: Buffered. If it fails or is weak: Lost on crash. Writeback Step What it does: Dirty pages. If it fails or is weak: Throttle. Block layer Step What it does: Queues. If it fails or is weak: Deep wait. NVMe device Data store What it does: Durable. If it fails or is weak: Slow fsync. Incident: Commits that took a second Incident A database showed slow commits while the average disk utilisation looked low. $ iostat -x 1 2 | tail -4 Device r/s w/s await aqu-sz %util nvme0n1 120 950 38.20 36.40 64.00 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The average utilisation looked acceptable, but the await value of thirty eight milliseconds and a queue of thirty six requests showed that writes waited behind a large background job that wrote at full speed. The team limited the job with the io controller, moved its output to another device and set an alert on await and queue depth. Commit latency fell to a few milliseconds. Common mistakes Judging a disk by utilisation alone Ignoring await and queue size Running heavy batch writes beside a database Assuming buffered write means durable Enabling io_uring without checking the security policy"},"linux-internals/container-runtime-internals-runc-overlayfs-seccomp-and-capabilities":{"html":"\u003cp>A container is an ordinary process with a restricted view and restricted rights. When you can name each restriction, debugging permission errors and security questions becomes much easier.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What runc does when it starts a container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>overlayfs: layers, copy up and whiteouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capabilities instead of a single root\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>seccomp filters and the system call surface\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging: why is this container allowed or denied\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-start-sequence\">The start sequence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A container runtime such as containerd prepares an image and a bundle, then runc creates new namespaces for the process: process IDs, mounts, network, hostname, inter process communication and sometimes users. It puts the process in cgroups, mounts the root file system, drops rights and executes the entry point. After that, runc exits and the process continues.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"overlayfs\">overlayfs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An overlay mounts several read only image layers under one writable layer. A read goes down through the layers until it finds the file. A write to a file from a lower layer first copies the whole file up, which can be slow for large files. Deleting a file records a whiteout marker. Keep heavy writes on a volume, not in the container layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capabilities\">Capabilities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Linux splits the powers of root into capabilities such as binding low ports, changing file ownership or loading modules. A container starts with a reduced default set. Drop all and add back only what is needed. A privileged container receives nearly everything and can reach the host, so avoid it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"seccomp\">seccomp\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A seccomp profile is a filter that allows, denies or logs system calls by number and arguments. The runtime default blocks dozens of dangerous calls, such as those that load modules or change the clock. A denied call usually returns an error such as operation not permitted, which looks like a permission problem but is a filter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debugging-access\">Debugging access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When something is denied, check in order: file permissions and user, capabilities, seccomp, then mandatory access control such as AppArmor or SELinux, then mounts and read only settings. Use strace to see the failing call and its error code, and inspect the effective capabilities in the process status file.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"rt&gt;ns;ns&gt;fs;fs&gt;sec\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Container runtime internals: runc, overlayfs, seccomp and capabilities\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rt\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runtime and runc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ns\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Namespaces and cgroups\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fs\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">overlayfs root\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Capabilities and seccomp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Runtime and runc to Namespaces and cgroups; 2, Namespaces and cgroups to overlayfs root; 3, overlayfs root to Capabilities and seccomp.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rt\">\u003cdt>Runtime and runc \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sets up the process.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad config.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ns\">\u003cdt>Namespaces and cgroups \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> View and limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Leaks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fs\">\u003cdt>overlayfs root \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Layers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Copy up cost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Capabilities and seccomp \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rights.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too broad.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-write-that-was-blocked-by-a-filter\">Incident: The write that was blocked by a filter\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A monitoring agent in a container failed with operation not permitted although it ran as root.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -E &quot;CapEff|Seccomp&quot; /proc/self/status\ncapsh --decode=00000000a80425fb | head -c 120\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;CapEff|Seccomp&quot;\u003c/span> /proc/self/status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CapEff: 00000000a80425fb\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Seccomp:        2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>capsh --decode=00000000a80425fb | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 120\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0x00000000a80425fb=cap_chown,cap_dac_override,cap_fowner,cap_fsetid,cap_kill\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The agent called a system call to read performance counters, which the default seccomp profile does not allow, and root in a container does not add the missing capability. The security team approved a narrow custom profile for that one system call and the capability it needed, applied only to that agent. They rejected the request to run it as a privileged container.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running containers as privileged\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing heavy data into the container layer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming root in a container has all rights\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling seccomp to make an error go away\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the capability drop step\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-start-sequence","text":"The start sequence"},{"id":"overlayfs","text":"overlayfs"},{"id":"capabilities","text":"Capabilities"},{"id":"seccomp","text":"seccomp"},{"id":"debugging-access","text":"Debugging access"},{"id":"incident-the-write-that-was-blocked-by-a-filter","text":"Incident: The write that was blocked by a filter"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux capabilities","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/capabilities.7.html"},{"t":"Seccomp BPF","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/userspace-api/seccomp_filter.html"},{"t":"Overlay filesystem","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/overlayfs.html"}],"text":"A container is an ordinary process with a restricted view and restricted rights. When you can name each restriction, debugging permission errors and security questions becomes much easier. What you will learn What runc does when it starts a container overlayfs: layers, copy up and whiteouts Capabilities instead of a single root seccomp filters and the system call surface Debugging: why is this container allowed or denied The start sequence A container runtime such as containerd prepares an image and a bundle, then runc creates new namespaces for the process: process IDs, mounts, network, hostname, inter process communication and sometimes users. It puts the process in cgroups, mounts the root file system, drops rights and executes the entry point. After that, runc exits and the process continues. overlayfs An overlay mounts several read only image layers under one writable layer. A read goes down through the layers until it finds the file. A write to a file from a lower layer first copies the whole file up, which can be slow for large files. Deleting a file records a whiteout marker. Keep heavy writes on a volume, not in the container layer. Capabilities Linux splits the powers of root into capabilities such as binding low ports, changing file ownership or loading modules. A container starts with a reduced default set. Drop all and add back only what is needed. A privileged container receives nearly everything and can reach the host, so avoid it. seccomp A seccomp profile is a filter that allows, denies or logs system calls by number and arguments. The runtime default blocks dozens of dangerous calls, such as those that load modules or change the clock. A denied call usually returns an error such as operation not permitted, which looks like a permission problem but is a filter. Debugging access When something is denied, check in order: file permissions and user, capabilities, seccomp, then mandatory access control such as AppArmor or SELinux, then mounts and read only settings. Use strace to see the failing call and its error code, and inspect the effective capabilities in the process status file. Container runtime internals: runc, overlayfs, seccomp and capabilities Flow in order: 1, Runtime and runc to Namespaces and cgroups; 2, Namespaces and cgroups to overlayfs root; 3, overlayfs root to Capabilities and seccomp. Step Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Runtime and runc Step What it does: Sets up the process. If it fails or is weak: Bad config. Namespaces and cgroups Security What it does: View and limits. If it fails or is weak: Leaks. overlayfs root Data store What it does: Layers. If it fails or is weak: Copy up cost. Capabilities and seccomp Security What it does: Rights. If it fails or is weak: Too broad. Incident: The write that was blocked by a filter Incident A monitoring agent in a container failed with operation not permitted although it ran as root. $ grep -E \"CapEff|Seccomp\" /proc/self/status CapEff: 00000000a80425fb Seccomp: 2 $ capsh --decode=00000000a80425fb | head -c 120 0x00000000a80425fb=cap_chown,cap_dac_override,cap_fowner,cap_fsetid,cap_kill (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The agent called a system call to read performance counters, which the default seccomp profile does not allow, and root in a container does not add the missing capability. The security team approved a narrow custom profile for that one system call and the capability it needed, applied only to that agent. They rejected the request to run it as a privileged container. Common mistakes Running containers as privileged Writing heavy data into the container layer Assuming root in a container has all rights Disabling seccomp to make an error go away Skipping the capability drop step"},"linux-internals/the-network-stack-path-netfilter-tcp-buffers-and-xdp":{"html":"\u003cp>Packet loss and slow connections often hide in a table that filled up or a buffer that was too small. Knowing the path tells you which counter to read.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The path from the card to the socket\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>netfilter hooks, nftables and conntrack\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>TCP buffers, windows and congestion control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Drops: where they happen and which counter shows them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>XDP and eBPF for early packet handling\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-path\">The path\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A packet arrives at the network card, which writes it to a receive ring in memory and raises an interrupt. The driver and the network stack process it in softirq, passing it through routing and filtering to a socket buffer, where the application reads it. On the way out the steps reverse. A drop can occur at the ring, in a filter, in the connection table or at the socket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"netfilter-and-conntrack\">netfilter and conntrack\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Netfilter provides hooks where rules can accept, drop or change packets, and nftables is the current rule language. Connection tracking records the state of each flow so that rules and address translation work. The table has a maximum size, and when it is full new connections are dropped with a log message. Services doing many short connections fill it quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tcp-buffers\">TCP buffers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each socket has send and receive buffers. The receive window tells the sender how much it may send, and a small buffer limits throughput on a path with high latency. The bandwidth delay product gives the buffer size needed. Auto tuning usually handles it, but limits set too low in sysctl can hold speed down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"congestion-control\">Congestion control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The congestion algorithm decides how fast a sender grows and shrinks its rate. Cubic is the common default, and BBR estimates bandwidth and delay and can do better on lossy or long paths. Retransmissions and a growing send queue visible in ss show problems. Change the algorithm only with measurements and for a clear path.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"xdp-and-ebpf\">XDP and eBPF\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>XDP runs a small verified program at the earliest point in the driver, before the full stack allocates buffers. It can drop, redirect or count packets at very high rates, which suits denial of service filtering and load balancing. It needs driver support and careful testing, and its programs are managed like code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"nic&gt;nf;nf&gt;tcp;tcp&gt;xdp\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The network stack path: netfilter, TCP buffers and XDP\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"nic\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Card and driver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"nf\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">netfilter and conntrack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tcp\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">TCP socket\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"xdp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">XDP program\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Card and driver to netfilter and conntrack; 2, netfilter and conntrack to TCP socket; 3, TCP socket to XDP program.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"nic\">\u003cdt>Card and driver \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ring buffer.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ring full.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"nf\">\u003cdt>netfilter and conntrack \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rules and state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Table full.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tcp\">\u003cdt>TCP socket \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Buffers and window.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Small buffer.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"xdp\">\u003cdt>XDP program \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Early drop.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad filter.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-new-connections-dropped-at-random\">Incident: New connections dropped at random\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A proxy host dropped about one in twenty new connections during peak traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dmesg | grep -i conntrack | tail -2\ncat /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_count /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_max\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dmesg | grep -i conntrack | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nf_conntrack: table full, dropping packet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_count /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_max\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">262113\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">262144\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The connection tracking table was full, because the proxy opened many short outbound connections and kept entries for a long timeout. The kernel dropped packets for new flows. The team raised the maximum with a matching memory check, shortened the timeouts for closed connections, reused upstream connections and added an alert when the table passed eighty percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ignoring conntrack table usage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing congestion control without measuring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Buffers too small for high latency paths\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Many short connections with no reuse\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using XDP without a rollback plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-path","text":"The path"},{"id":"netfilter-and-conntrack","text":"netfilter and conntrack"},{"id":"tcp-buffers","text":"TCP buffers"},{"id":"congestion-control","text":"Congestion control"},{"id":"xdp-and-ebpf","text":"XDP and eBPF"},{"id":"incident-new-connections-dropped-at-random","text":"Incident: New connections dropped at random"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Networking documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/networking/index.html"},{"t":"nftables wiki","u":"https://wiki.nftables.org/wiki-nftables/index.php/Main_Page"},{"t":"Open Container runtime specification","u":"https://github.com/opencontainers/runtime-spec"}],"text":"Packet loss and slow connections often hide in a table that filled up or a buffer that was too small. Knowing the path tells you which counter to read. What you will learn The path from the card to the socket netfilter hooks, nftables and conntrack TCP buffers, windows and congestion control Drops: where they happen and which counter shows them XDP and eBPF for early packet handling The path A packet arrives at the network card, which writes it to a receive ring in memory and raises an interrupt. The driver and the network stack process it in softirq, passing it through routing and filtering to a socket buffer, where the application reads it. On the way out the steps reverse. A drop can occur at the ring, in a filter, in the connection table or at the socket. netfilter and conntrack Netfilter provides hooks where rules can accept, drop or change packets, and nftables is the current rule language. Connection tracking records the state of each flow so that rules and address translation work. The table has a maximum size, and when it is full new connections are dropped with a log message. Services doing many short connections fill it quickly. TCP buffers Each socket has send and receive buffers. The receive window tells the sender how much it may send, and a small buffer limits throughput on a path with high latency. The bandwidth delay product gives the buffer size needed. Auto tuning usually handles it, but limits set too low in sysctl can hold speed down. Congestion control The congestion algorithm decides how fast a sender grows and shrinks its rate. Cubic is the common default, and BBR estimates bandwidth and delay and can do better on lossy or long paths. Retransmissions and a growing send queue visible in ss show problems. Change the algorithm only with measurements and for a clear path. XDP and eBPF XDP runs a small verified program at the earliest point in the driver, before the full stack allocates buffers. It can drop, redirect or count packets at very high rates, which suits denial of service filtering and load balancing. It needs driver support and careful testing, and its programs are managed like code. The network stack path: netfilter, TCP buffers and XDP Flow in order: 1, Card and driver to netfilter and conntrack; 2, netfilter and conntrack to TCP socket; 3, TCP socket to XDP program. Outside party Security Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Card and driver Outside party What it does: Ring buffer. If it fails or is weak: Ring full. netfilter and conntrack Security What it does: Rules and state. If it fails or is weak: Table full. TCP socket Service What it does: Buffers and window. If it fails or is weak: Small buffer. XDP program Step What it does: Early drop. If it fails or is weak: Bad filter. Incident: New connections dropped at random Incident A proxy host dropped about one in twenty new connections during peak traffic. $ dmesg | grep -i conntrack | tail -2 nf_conntrack: table full, dropping packet $ cat /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_count /proc/sys/net/netfilter/nf_conntrack_max 262113 262144 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The connection tracking table was full, because the proxy opened many short outbound connections and kept entries for a long timeout. The kernel dropped packets for new flows. The team raised the maximum with a matching memory check, shortened the timeouts for closed connections, reused upstream connections and added an alert when the table passed eighty percent. Common mistakes Ignoring conntrack table usage Changing congestion control without measuring Buffers too small for high latency paths Many short connections with no reuse Using XDP without a rollback plan"}});